(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "7.6", title: "Python 1: first text-based programs", q: "How do I turn an algorithm into a program a computer can run?", lessons: [
{ t: "From Scratch to Python", o: "I can explain how a text-based language differs from blocks and run my first Python program.",
  k: ["Python: a text-based programming language", "program: a set of instructions written in a programming language", "run: to make the computer carry out a program"],
  d: ["In Scratch, which block shows a message on the screen?", "say (for example say Hello for 2 seconds)."],
  s: ["Same ideas, new form|Scratch: drag blocks that snap together|Python: type the instructions as text|Sequence, selection, iteration and variables all still apply",
      "Side by side|Scratch: `say Hello`|Python:|>> print('Hello')|The computer does exactly what is typed",
      "Running a program|Type the code in the editor|Save it with a name ending in .py|Press Run and read the output"],
  a: "Open the Python editor, type the three-line starter program exactly, save it as `hello.py` and run it. Then match the six Scratch blocks to their Python lines. Support: follow the screenshot guide. Stretch: change the program to print a short poem of four lines.",
  w: [["What is Python?", "A text-based programming language."], ["Which Python instruction displays a message?", "print."], ["What file extension does a Python program have?", ".py"], ["Write the Python line that displays: Good morning", "print('Good morning')"], ["Give one advantage of blocks and one advantage of text for writing programs.", "Blocks cannot be mistyped and are easy to start with. Text is faster to write for large programs and is what professional languages use."]],
  x: ["What do you press to make the computer carry out your program?", "Run."] },
{ t: "print and the rules of syntax", o: "I can use print correctly and explain what syntax is.",
  k: ["syntax: the rules for how code must be written", "string: text inside quotation marks", "syntax error: a mistake that breaks the rules, so the program cannot run"],
  d: ["Spot the mistake: `print('Hello'`", "The closing bracket is missing."],
  s: ["Syntax|Every language has rules of spelling and punctuation|Python checks them before it runs anything|One missing bracket stops the whole program",
      "The rules of print|Lower-case `print`|Brackets around what to show|Quotation marks around text|>> print('I am learning Python')",
      "More with print|Several items, separated by commas:|>> print('Score:', 10)|An empty `print()` leaves a blank line"],
  a: "Fix the ten broken print lines on the sheet, naming the rule each one breaks. Then write a program that prints a three-line menu with a blank line after the title. Support: fix the first five, which each have one missing symbol. Stretch: print a box made of symbols around your name.",
  w: [["What is syntax?", "The rules for how code must be written."], ["What is a string?", "Text inside quotation marks."], ["Fix: `Print('Hi')`", "print('Hi') with a lower-case p."], ["Fix: `print(Hello)`", "print('Hello'): text needs quotation marks."], ["Explain why Python cannot run a program that has one missing bracket.", "It cannot tell where the instruction ends, so it stops with a syntax error before running anything."]],
  x: ["Name the three things every print line needs when showing text.", "The word print in lower case, brackets, quotation marks."] },
{ t: "Lab: predict, run and modify greeting programs", o: "I can predict what a Python program will do and modify it.",
  k: ["predict: to say what will happen before running", "output: what the program displays", "modify: to change a program so it does something different"],
  d: ["What will this display?\n`print('A')`\n`print('B')`", "A, then B on the next line."],
  s: ["Predict|Read each line in order|Write the output exactly as you expect it|Then run it",
      "Investigate|Was the prediction right?|Change one thing and run again|What happens if you delete a quotation mark?",
      "Modify and make|Change the words|Change the order of the lines|Add new lines of your own"],
  a: "Work through the five starter programs: predict, run, investigate, modify. Then make a 'business card' program that prints your name, class, three interests and a border. Support: complete programs 1 to 3. Stretch: use `print('=' * 20)` and work out what it does.",
  w: [["Predict the output: `print('Hello', 'world')`", "Hello world"], ["Predict the output: `print('1 + 1')`", "1 + 1 (it is text, so it is not calculated)."], ["Predict the output: `print(1 + 1)`", "2"], ["Which line runs first in a Python program?", "The top line."], ["Explain why `print('2 + 3')` and `print(2 + 3)` give different outputs.", "With quotation marks it is a string and is shown as typed. Without them it is a calculation, so Python works out 5."]],
  x: ["What does `print('-' * 5)` display?", "-----"] },
{ t: "Variables and assignment", o: "I can store values in variables and use them.",
  k: ["variable: a named place in memory that stores a value", "assignment: giving a variable a value using =", "identifier: the name of a variable"],
  d: ["In Scratch, which block gave Score a starting value?", "set Score to 0."],
  s: ["Creating a variable|>> score = 0|>> name = 'Sam'|Read = as 'becomes', not 'equals'",
      "Using a variable|>> print(name)|>> print('Score:', score)|No quotation marks around a variable name",
      "Changing a variable|>> score = score + 1|Work out the right-hand side first, then store it|Good names: lower case, no spaces, say what is stored: `high_score`"],
  a: "Complete the trace tables for the four short programs, then write a program with variables for your name, age and favourite subject that prints a sentence using all three. Support: fill in the gaps in the part-written program. Stretch: swap the values of two variables using a third.",
  w: [["What is a variable?", "A named place in memory that stores a value."], ["Write the line that stores 12 in a variable called `age`.", "age = 12"], ["After `x = 5` and `x = x + 2`, what is x?", "7"], ["Which is the better variable name: `n` or `player_name`? Why?", "player_name, because it says what is stored."], ["Explain why `print('score')` and `print(score)` are different.", "The first displays the word score. The second displays the value stored in the variable score."]],
  x: ["How should the = sign be read in Python?", "As 'becomes' (assignment)."] },
{ t: "input and strings", o: "I can ask the user a question and use their answer.",
  k: ["input: data the user types in while the program runs", "prompt: the message that tells the user what to type", "concatenation: joining strings together with +"],
  d: ["How does a program become different each time it runs?", "By asking the user for input."],
  s: ["Asking a question|>> name = input('What is your name? ')|The prompt is shown, the program waits, and the answer is stored|Put a space at the end of the prompt",
      "Using the answer|>> print('Hello', name)|Or join strings with +|>> print('Hello ' + name + '!')",
      "Take care|input always gives a string, even if digits are typed|Strings can only be joined to other strings with +|Commas in print are simpler and add spaces for you"],
  a: "Write a program that asks for the user's name, town and favourite food, then prints two sentences using all three answers. Support: complete the half-written program. Stretch: print the name in upper case using `.upper()` and report how many letters it has using `len()`.",
  w: [["What does input do?", "It shows a prompt, waits for the user to type, and gives back what they typed."], ["Write a line that asks 'Favourite colour? ' and stores the answer in `colour`.", "colour = input('Favourite colour? ')"], ["What is concatenation?", "Joining strings together with +."], ["`name = 'Ali'`. What does `print('Hi ' + name)` display?", "Hi Ali"], ["Explain why the result of input needs to be stored in a variable.", "Otherwise the answer is lost as soon as it is typed and cannot be used later."]],
  x: ["What data type does input always give?", "A string."] },
{ t: "Lab: Mad Libs story generator", o: "I can build a program that uses several inputs to create output.",
  k: ["template: a pattern with gaps to be filled in", "test: to run a program with chosen inputs to check it", "user-friendly: easy and pleasant to use"],
  d: ["A story has gaps for a noun, a verb and an adjective. What must the program do first?", "Ask the user for each word and store it."],
  s: ["Plan|Write a four-sentence story with five gaps|Decide a variable name for each gap|Write the prompts",
      "Build|Ask for all the inputs first|Then print the story using the variables|Run it after every two lines",
      "Polish|Clear prompts: 'Type an animal: '|A title and a blank line before the story|Test with silly and sensible words"],
  a: "Create a Mad Libs program with at least five inputs and a four-sentence story. Test it twice and let a partner play. Support: use the story template with the gaps marked. Stretch: ask whether the user wants to see the story in capitals and print it that way.",
  w: [["How many variables does a story with five gaps need?", "Five."], ["Write a clear prompt for asking for an adjective.", "For example input('Type an adjective (a describing word): ')"], ["Why should you run the program after every few lines?", "Errors are easier to find when only a little code is new."], ["`animal = input('Animal: ')`. Write a print line that uses it in a sentence.", "For example print('The', animal, 'ran away.')"], ["Explain what makes a program user-friendly.", "Clear prompts, tidy output, and behaviour the user expects."]],
  x: ["Which was more common in your program: syntax errors or wrong output?", "A reflection."] },
{ t: "Integers, floats and casting", o: "I can use the correct data type and convert between types.",
  k: ["integer: a whole number", "float: a number with a decimal point", "casting: converting a value from one data type to another"],
  d: ["What do you think `print('5' + '5')` displays?", "55, because the two strings are joined."],
  s: ["Data types|String `str`: 'hello', '42'|Integer `int`: 42, -7|Float `float`: 3.14, 2.0",
      "The input problem|input gives a string, so '5' + '5' is '55'|Convert it before doing maths|>> age = int(input('Age: '))",
      "Casting|`int('12')` gives 12|`float('2.5')` gives 2.5|`str(12)` gives '12', ready to join to other text"],
  a: "Predict and test the ten casting lines, then write a program that asks for two whole numbers and prints their sum, and another that asks for a height in metres as a decimal. Support: fix the program that adds '5' and '5' to make 55. Stretch: find out what `int('3.7')` and `int(3.7)` do and explain the difference.",
  w: [["What is an integer?", "A whole number."], ["What is a float?", "A number with a decimal point."], ["What does `int('8') + int('2')` give?", "10"], ["Write a line that asks for the user's age and stores it as an integer.", "age = int(input('Age: '))"], ["A program asks for two numbers and prints `35` when the user types 3 and 5. Explain the bug and the fix.", "The inputs are strings, so + joins them. Cast each to int before adding."]],
  x: ["Which function turns a number into a string?", "str()"] },
{ t: "Arithmetic operators", o: "I can use Python's arithmetic operators, including integer division and remainder.",
  k: ["operator: a symbol that performs a calculation", "integer division: division that gives a whole-number answer, written //", "modulus: the remainder after division, written %"],
  d: ["Share 7 sweets between 2 people. How many each, and how many left over?", "3 each, 1 left over."],
  s: ["The operators|`+` add, `-` subtract, `*` multiply, `/` divide|`**` power: `2 ** 3` is 8|`/` always gives a float: `10 / 4` is 2.5",
      "Whole-number division|`7 // 2` is 3: how many whole times|`7 % 2` is 1: the remainder|Together they answer sharing problems",
      "Order of operations|Brackets first, then powers, then * and /, then + and -|`2 + 3 * 4` is 14|`(2 + 3) * 4` is 20"],
  a: "Predict then test the 12 calculations. Write a program that asks for a number of minutes and prints it as hours and minutes using // and %. Support: complete the calculation table with a partner. Stretch: write a program that says whether a number is odd or even using %.",
  w: [["What does `**` do?", "Raises to a power."], ["What is `17 // 5`?", "3"], ["What is `17 % 5`?", "2"], ["What is `2 + 3 * 4`?", "14"], ["Explain how // and % turn 135 minutes into hours and minutes.", "135 // 60 gives 2 hours and 135 % 60 gives 15 minutes left over."]],
  x: ["What is `10 / 4` and what data type is the answer?", "2.5, a float."] },
{ t: "Lab: unit converter", o: "I can write a program that takes input, calculates and outputs a result.",
  k: ["formula: a rule for calculating something", "round: to shorten a number to a set number of decimal places", "input-process-output: the pattern most simple programs follow"],
  d: ["To convert kilometres to metres, what do you multiply by?", "1000."],
  s: ["Plan with input-process-output|Input: the value to convert|Process: apply the formula|Output: the result with its unit",
      "Example: Celsius to Fahrenheit|>> c = float(input('Celsius: '))|>> f = c * 9 / 5 + 32|>> print(c, 'C is', f, 'F')",
      "Polish|`round(f, 1)` gives one decimal place|Use float so decimals are accepted|Test with values you can check by hand: 0 C is 32 F, 100 C is 212 F"],
  a: "Build a converter for temperature, then add a second conversion of your choice (currency, distance or weight). Test each with three values and record expected and actual results. Then check your temperature converter with the Python check page of CS Toolkit. Support: complete the converter with the formula line missing. Stretch: add a menu so the user chooses which conversion to run.",
  w: [["Name the three parts of the input-process-output pattern.", "Input, process, output."], ["Why use float and not int for a temperature?", "Temperatures can have decimal parts, and int would cause an error or lose them."], ["What does `round(3.14159, 2)` give?", "3.14"], ["Write the line that converts `km` to metres and stores it in `m`.", "m = km * 1000"], ["Explain why 0 and 100 are good test values for a Celsius to Fahrenheit converter.", "The correct answers, 32 and 212, are well known, so mistakes are easy to spot."]],
  x: ["What did your converter output for 36.6 C?", "97.88 F (or 97.9 if rounded)."] },
{ t: "Reading error messages", o: "I can read a Python error message and find the line it refers to.",
  k: ["error message: text Python displays when something goes wrong", "traceback: the part of an error message that shows where the error happened", "NameError: the error for using a name Python does not know"],
  d: ["When a program crashes, what is the first thing you should read?", "The last line of the error message, then the line number."],
  s: ["Errors are help, not failure|Every programmer sees them every day|The message says what went wrong and where|Read it before changing anything",
      "How to read one|Last line: the type of error and a description|Above it: the line number|The mistake is on that line or the one just before",
      "Three common errors|`SyntaxError`: a rule of the language is broken|`NameError`: a name is misspelt or was never created|`TypeError`: the wrong data type, such as adding a string to a number"],
  a: "For each of the eight error messages on the sheet, identify the error type, the line number and the likely cause, then match it to the broken program that produced it. Support: match the first four using the error type only. Stretch: write three short programs that each cause a different error on purpose.",
  w: [["Which line of an error message tells you the type of error?", "The last line."], ["What causes a NameError?", "Using a name Python does not know, usually a misspelling or a variable not yet created."], ["What causes a TypeError?", "Using the wrong data type, such as adding a string to a number."], ["`print(nmae)` gives an error when the variable is called `name`. Which error?", "NameError."], ["Explain why the mistake is sometimes on the line before the one in the error message.", "A missing bracket or quotation mark is only noticed when Python reaches the next line."]],
  x: ["What two things should you read first in an error message?", "The error type on the last line, and the line number."] },
{ t: "A debugging routine", o: "I can follow a routine to find and fix errors on my own.",
  k: ["debug: to find and fix errors in a program", "logic error: the program runs but gives the wrong result", "retest: to run the program again after a change"],
  d: ["A program runs without crashing but says 2 + 2 is 22. Is that a syntax error?", "No. It runs, so it is a logic error (strings were joined)."],
  s: ["Read, locate, fix, retest|Read the message|Locate the line|Fix one thing|Retest with the same input",
      "Logic errors|No error message appears|The output is simply wrong|Find them by predicting, then comparing with the real output",
      "Three before me|Reread the message and the line|Check your notes or a similar program|Ask your partner, then the teacher"],
  a: "Debug the six programs: three have syntax errors, two have a NameError or TypeError, and one has a logic error. For each, record the routine you followed. Support: the faulty line is marked in the first three. Stretch: the last program has three bugs. Find all of them.",
  w: [["Name the four steps of the debugging routine.", "Read, locate, fix, retest."], ["What is a logic error?", "The program runs but gives the wrong result."], ["Why change only one thing before retesting?", "So you know which change fixed, or broke, the program."], ["A program should add VAT of 20% but multiplies the price by 20. What kind of error is this?", "A logic error."], ["Explain why logic errors are harder to find than syntax errors.", "Python gives no message. You have to notice that the output is wrong and work out why."]],
  x: ["What does 'three before me' mean?", "Try three ways to solve the problem yourself before asking the teacher."] },
{ t: "Lab: error-message bingo", o: "I can fix a range of broken programs quickly and name each error.",
  k: ["IndentationError: the error for spaces at the start of a line being wrong", "ValueError: the error for a value that cannot be converted, such as int('abc')", "fluent: able to do something quickly and correctly"],
  d: ["What error does `int('hello')` cause?", "ValueError."],
  s: ["The game|Each card shows nine error types|Run a broken program, read the message|Cross off the error and fix the program",
      "Two new errors|`ValueError`: `int('abc')` cannot be converted|`IndentationError`: unexpected spaces at the start of a line|Both name the line",
      "Keep a bug diary|Error type|What caused it|How you fixed it"],
  a: "Play error bingo with the ten broken programs: run, read, name the error, fix and retest. Record each one in your bug diary. Support: work in a pair on the first five. Stretch: write a 'top five mistakes and how to avoid them' guide for next year's Year 7.",
  w: [["What causes a ValueError?", "A value that cannot be converted, such as int('abc')."], ["What causes an IndentationError?", "Wrong or unexpected spaces at the start of a line."], ["Name the error: `print('Hi)`", "SyntaxError (missing closing quotation mark)."], ["Name the error: `age = input('Age: ')` then `print(age + 1)`", "TypeError (a string and a number cannot be added)."], ["Explain how a bug diary helps you become a better programmer.", "You notice the mistakes you repeat and learn their fixes, so you solve them faster next time."]],
  x: ["Which error type did you meet most often today?", "A named error."] },
{ t: "Comparison operators and if-else", o: "I can write a program that makes a decision with if and else.",
  k: ["condition: an expression that is True or False", "comparison operator: a symbol that compares two values, such as > or ==", "indentation: spaces at the start of a line that show it belongs inside a block"],
  d: ["In a flowchart, which symbol asks a yes or no question?", "The decision (diamond)."],
  s: ["Comparison operators|`==` equal to, `!=` not equal to|`>` greater than, `<` less than|`>=` and `<=` include the boundary",
      "if and else|>> if mark >= 50:|>>     print('Pass')|>> else:|>>     print('Fail')",
      "The rules|A colon at the end of the `if` and `else` lines|The lines inside are indented by four spaces|`=` assigns; `==` compares"],
  a: "Write three programs: one that says whether a number is positive, one that checks whether a password matches, and one that decides whether someone is old enough for a ride. Test each at the boundary. Support: add the missing colons and indentation to the part-written programs. Stretch: use `%` to print whether a number is odd or even.",
  w: [["What is a condition?", "An expression that is True or False."], ["What is the difference between = and ==?", "= assigns a value to a variable. == compares two values."], ["What must be at the end of an `if` line?", "A colon."], ["Write an if-else that prints 'Adult' when `age` is 18 or more, otherwise 'Child'.", "if age >= 18:\n    print('Adult')\nelse:\n    print('Child')"], ["Explain what indentation tells Python.", "Which lines belong inside the if or else block and should run only when that branch is chosen."]],
  x: ["Is `7 >= 7` True or False?", "True."] },
{ t: "elif: more than two choices", o: "I can use elif to choose between several options.",
  k: ["elif: short for 'else if', it tests another condition when the ones above were False", "branch: one of the routes a program can take", "boundary: the value where the outcome changes"],
  d: ["A grade can be A, B, C or U. How many decisions does a flowchart need?", "Three decisions give four outcomes."],
  s: ["Why elif?|if-else gives two routes|elif adds more, tested in order from the top|Only the first True branch runs",
      "Example|>> if mark >= 80:|>>     print('Grade A')|>> elif mark >= 60:|>>     print('Grade B')|>> else:|>>     print('Ungraded')",
      "Order matters|Test the highest boundary first|If `mark >= 60` came first, 95 would print Grade B|`else` catches everything left over"],
  a: "Write a grade calculator: A for 80 or more, B for 60 to 79, C for 40 to 59, otherwise Ungraded. Test at 80, 79, 60, 40 and 39, then check it on the Python check page of CS Toolkit. Support: complete the program with one elif missing. Stretch: print 'Invalid' for marks below 0 or above 100.",
  w: [["What does elif mean?", "Else if: test another condition when the ones above were False."], ["How many branches can run in one if-elif-else chain?", "One."], ["In the grade program, what is printed for 79?", "Grade B."], ["Why must `mark >= 80` be tested before `mark >= 60`?", "Otherwise marks of 80 or more would match the 60 test first and get Grade B."], ["Explain the difference between three separate `if` statements and one if-elif-else chain.", "Separate ifs are all tested, so several can run. In a chain only the first True branch runs."]],
  x: ["Which five marks should you test for the grade calculator, and why?", "80, 79, 60, 40, 39 (accept 59): the boundaries where the grade changes."] },
{ t: "Lab: quiz program with a score", o: "I can combine input, selection and a variable to build a quiz.",
  k: ["score: a variable that counts correct answers", "increment: to add one to a variable", "case-insensitive: treating capital and small letters as the same"],
  d: ["A quiz adds 1 to the score for each correct answer. Write that line in Python.", "score = score + 1"],
  s: ["Plan|Set `score = 0` at the start|For each question: ask, compare, respond|Print the final score at the end",
      "One question|>> answer = input('Capital of France? ')|>> if answer == 'Paris':|>>     score = score + 1|>>     print('Correct')|>> else:|>>     print('It is Paris')",
      "Make it fair|`answer.lower() == 'paris'` accepts any capitals|For number answers, cast to int|Test right and wrong answers for every question"],
  a: "Build a five-question quiz on a topic you choose, with a score and a final message that depends on the score. Test every question with a right and a wrong answer. Support: start from the one-question program and copy the pattern. Stretch: use elif to give three different final messages.",
  w: [["Why is `score = 0` placed at the start of the program?", "So the score begins at zero before any question is asked."], ["Write the line that adds 1 to `score`.", "score = score + 1"], ["A user types 'paris' but the program expects 'Paris'. What happens, and how is it fixed?", "It is marked wrong. Compare answer.lower() with 'paris'."], ["How many tests does a five-question quiz need to check every branch?", "Ten: a right and a wrong answer for each question."], ["Explain where the line that prints the final score must go, and why.", "After all the questions, not indented, so it runs once at the end whatever the answers were."]],
  x: ["What was the hardest bug in your quiz?", "A specific bug."] },
{ t: "Plan a mini-project", o: "I can plan a program before I write it.",
  k: ["plan: a description of what a program will do and how", "pseudocode: a plan written in structured English, not a real language", "success criteria: what the finished program must do"],
  d: ["Why plan a program before typing any code?", "To find problems early and know what to build, in what order."],
  s: ["Choose|A chatbot that asks questions and responds|Or a calculator that offers several operations|At least 15 lines, with input, a calculation and a decision",
      "Plan it|List the inputs, the processing and the outputs|Write the steps in order, in plain English|Mark where the decision is and what its condition will be",
      "Success criteria|Write four 'The program must...' statements|Choose three sets of test data, including a boundary|Predict the output for each"],
  a: "Complete the project planning sheet: inputs, outputs, numbered steps, the decision and its condition, four success criteria and three tests with expected results. Support: use the chatbot planning frame. Stretch: plan an extra feature that uses elif.",
  w: [["Which project did you choose?", "Chatbot or calculator."], ["List your program's inputs and outputs.", "Suitable lists."], ["Write the condition your decision will use.", "A valid condition."], ["Write two success criteria.", "Two testable statements."], ["Give one test value and the output you expect.", "A test with an expected result."]],
  x: ["What is the first line of code you will write next lesson?", "A sensible first line, such as an input."] },
{ t: "Assessed build", o: "I can write, test and debug a program that meets a plan.",
  k: ["build: to write the program", "test evidence: a record of tests and results", "independent: working without help"],
  d: ["Name the four steps of the debugging routine.", "Read, locate, fix, retest."],
  s: ["The task|Build the program you planned|Work on your own; notes are allowed|Save after every working step",
      "Order of work|Inputs first, then the calculation, then the decision|Run after every two or three lines|Add comments beginning with #",
      "Test evidence|Run your three planned tests|Record expected and actual output|Fix anything that does not match"],
  a: "Write your planned program, test it with your three sets of test data and complete the test table. Hand in the code and the table. Support: use your bug diary and the syntax help card. Stretch: add input checking so the program copes with unexpected answers.",
  w: [["Does your program use input, a calculation and a decision? Give the line number of each.", "Three line numbers."], ["Record your first test: input, expected output, actual output.", "A complete test record."], ["Describe one error you met and how you fixed it.", "An error and its fix."], ["Write one comment from your code.", "A meaningful comment."], ["Which success criterion is not fully met, and what would you do next?", "An honest evaluation."]],
  x: ["How many of your tests passed?", "A number out of 3."] },
{ t: "Feedback, code walkthrough and reflection", o: "I can explain my code and reflect on a year of Computing.",
  k: ["walkthrough: explaining a program line by line", "feedback: comments that help you improve", "reflection: thinking about what and how you have learned"],
  d: ["Choose the three lines of your program you are proudest of.", "The student's own choice."],
  s: ["Walkthrough|Explain your program to a partner, line by line|Use the words variable, input, condition, branch|Your partner asks one 'what if' question",
      "Improve|Read your feedback|Make the suggested change and retest|Save as the final version",
      "The year in review|Safety, algorithms, Scratch, binary, hardware, Python|Which ideas kept coming back?|Next year: loops in Python, networks, web pages"],
  a: "Carry out a code walkthrough with a partner, improve your program using the feedback, then complete the end-of-year reflection. Support: use the walkthrough sentence starters. Stretch: write the main decision of your program as a flowchart and as a Scratch script to show it is the same idea.",
  w: [["Copy your next step and say what you changed.", "The feedback and the change."], ["Explain one line of your code using the correct terms.", "An accurate explanation."], ["What 'what if' question did your partner ask, and what was the answer?", "A question and answer."], ["Which unit this year did you enjoy most, and why?", "A reasoned choice."], ["Set one target for Year 8 Computing.", "A specific target."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, how do you turn an algorithm into a program?", "Write each step in the syntax of a programming language, then test and debug until it does what the algorithm describes."] }
] }); })(typeof module !== "undefined" ? require("../lessons.js") : window.Toolkit);
